# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 2: Person Description
#
# The WIEN task for the Christmas-market photo of sub-lesson 1: who is present,
# and 1–2 of them described visually. The examiner's second prompt on the picture
# task is almost always "pick one person and describe them" — clothes, what they
# are doing, roughly how old they look — so this drills exactly that.
#
# The photo detail this describes: two men standing at the stall's counter, both
# in dark winter jackets and light trousers, ordering food; in the foreground a
# girl with her hair in a ponytail wearing a blue fluffy hooded jacket, and next
# to her a smaller child in a bright yellow high-visibility vest.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION, same as 01 — no @question prompts, no first-person opinion.
# A depicted person's visible state (`Si gesäit kal aus`) is observation and is
# wanted: both children are dressed for cold weather and that is checkable
# against the photo.
#
# Same A1–A2 constraint as the rest of the theme, with the guard that appearance
# descriptions make easy to break: attributive adjectives are used ONLY on
# feminine nouns (`eng blo Jackett`), where Luxembourgish leaves the adjective
# uninflected. Masculine and neuter attributives need declension the learner has
# not met, so those are stated predicatively instead (`De Mantel ass donkel.`).
#
# Words sub-lesson 1 already teaches (d'Leit, d'Kanner, d'Bild, gesinn, lénks,
# riets, …) are deliberately NOT repeated — the theme's pass-gate makes 01 a
# prerequisite for 02, and stat keys are shared across the app. Overlap with the
# other picture themes (de Mann, jonk, donkel) is accepted: themes are
# independent and all open, so a learner may reach this one first.
#
# Ten @fill blocks carry this file's share of the answering-pattern drill: the
# split `gesäit … aus` state frame, the `hunn … un` clothing frame for both a
# masculine and a feminine garment, the "describing the person on the … side"
# opener, the relative clause, the possessive appearance frame, and the numeral
# pair — most of the person-description library, each reused with a pairing or
# blank no earlier picture theme has taught for THIS photo's people.

@lesson P3.02 "Chrëschtmaart: Person Description"

# Same photo as 01, declared again: @image is per-file, and the learner must see
# the photo during THIS sub-lesson too. The alt text differs on purpose — it
# describes the detail this file drills.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/chreschtmaart/img/chreschtmaart.webp"
@image-alt "The same Christmas-market photo, seen for the people in it: two men in dark winter jackets ordering at the counter, a girl with a ponytail in a blue fluffy hooded jacket in the foreground, and next to her a smaller child in a bright yellow high-visibility vest."

# --- People ---
@word de Mann = the man
@word d'Männer = the men
@word d'Meedchen = the girl
@word d'Kand = the child

# --- Clothes ---
@word de Mantel = the coat
@word d'Jackett = the jacket
@word d'Kaputz = the hood
@word d'Box = the trousers

# --- Colours ---
@word donkel = dark
@word hell = light
@word blo = blue
@word giel = yellow

# --- What they are doing ---
@word ausgesinn = to look
@word niewent = next to
@word hunn = to have

# --- Describing them ---
@word jonk = young
@word kal = cold
@word glécklech = happy
@word ongeféier = approximately

@sentence
@lu Zwee Männer stinn um Stand a bestellen.
@en Two men are standing at the stall and ordering.
@distractor-lu waarden
@distractor-lu Kanner
@distractor-en waiting
@distractor-en children

@sentence
@lu De Mann lénks huet e Mantel un an de Mantel ass donkel.
@en The man on the left is wearing a coat and the coat is dark.
@distractor-lu hell
@distractor-lu Kaputz
@distractor-en light
@distractor-en hood

@sentence
@lu Hir Box ass hell.
@en Their trousers are light.
@distractor-lu donkel
@distractor-lu Mantel
@distractor-en dark
@distractor-en coat

@sentence
@lu Am Vierdergrond ass e Meedchen mat engem Kand.
@en In the foreground there is a girl with a child.
@distractor-lu Mann
@distractor-lu Stand
@distractor-en man
@distractor-en stall

@sentence
@lu D'Meedchen huet eng blo Jackett mat enger Kaputz un.
@en The girl is wearing a blue jacket with a hood.
@distractor-lu giel
@distractor-lu Box
@distractor-en yellow
@distractor-en trousers

@sentence
@lu D'Kand niewent hir huet eng giel Jackett un.
@en The child next to her is wearing a yellow jacket.
@distractor-lu blo
@distractor-lu Mantel
@distractor-en blue
@distractor-en coat

@sentence
@lu D'Meedchen ass jonk an et gesäit kal aus.
@en The girl is young and looks cold.
@distractor-lu al
@distractor-lu waarm
@distractor-en old
@distractor-en warm

@sentence
@lu D'Kanner sinn ongeféier fënnef Joer al.
@en The children are approximately five years old.
@distractor-lu zéng
@distractor-lu Männer
@distractor-en ten
@distractor-en men

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The visible-state frame with the split `gesäit … aus`. The verb bracket is the
# pattern being taught, so it stays in the frame and the adjective is the blank.
# Both distractors are states the thick jacket and hood contradict, so the item
# does not rest on grammar alone.
@fill
@lu D'Meedchen gesäit [kal] aus.
@en The girl looks [cold].
@distractor-lu waarm
@distractor-lu midd
@distractor-en warm
@distractor-en tired

# The `hunn … un` clothing frame for a masculine garment — the separable `un`
# sits at the end, an order the learner reads rather than builds. `e` is fixed in
# the frame and masculine, so feminine `Jackett` and `Box` are ruled out on
# grammar alone (R4) as well as on the photo.
@fill
@lu Déi zwee Männer hunn e [Mantel] un.
@en The two men are wearing a [coat].
@distractor-lu Jackett
@distractor-lu Box
@distractor-en jacket
@distractor-en trousers

# The same clothing frame with a feminine garment and determiner, so the
# opposite gender rules out `Mantel` and `Box` this time — R4 doing the work from
# the other side. `eng` is fixed and feminine.
@fill
@lu D'Meedchen huet eng [Jackett] un.
@en The girl is wearing a [jacket].
@distractor-lu Mantel
@distractor-lu Kaputz
@distractor-en coat
@distractor-en hood

# The "describing the person on the … side" opener from groussgaass, reused for
# a different person and side. `lénkser` is declined and stays in the fixed
# frame (R4) — blanking it would ask the learner to produce a form nothing
# teaches. `de` outside the blank leaves only `de`-taking nouns, and both
# distractors are wrong about who stands there.
@fill
@lu Ech beschreiwen de [Mann] op der lénkser Säit.
@en I am describing the [man] on the left side.
@distractor-lu Meedchen
@distractor-lu Kand
@distractor-en girl
@distractor-en child

# The relative-clause frame from Schueberfouer, reused with a market verb.
# `deen … bestellt` is verb-final, so the learner reads the order instead of
# assembling it, and the comma ends the clause, which is what keeps the blank
# after `Mann` safe under the Eifeler Regel. Blank is the verb: a noun cannot
# fill a verb slot at all (R3).
@fill
@lu Ech gesinn e Mann, deen um Stand [bestellt].
@en I see a man who [is ordering] at the stall.
@distractor-lu Kand
@distractor-lu Jackett
@distractor-en child
@distractor-en jacket

# The numeral-plus-noun pair for the market's two men. `Beim Stand sinn` puts
# the finite verb before both blanks, so the numeral tile is the word directly
# in front of the noun blank rather than `sinn` — `zwee` does not end in `-n`,
# which is what keeps the frame clear of the Eifeler Regel (R5). Different word
# classes in the two blanks (numeral, then plural noun), so they cannot swap
# (R6).
@fill
@lu Beim Stand sinn et [zwee] [Männer].
@en At the stall there are [two] [men].
@distractor-lu Kanner
@distractor-lu Meedercher
@distractor-en children
@distractor-en girls

# The possessive appearance frame — `Hiren/Säin … ass …` recurs for any person,
# here applied to the girl's jacket colour rather than hair. One blank on
# purpose: the two-blank version would join two predicative adjectives with no
# forced order, an R6 violation (see the library's hair-colour precedent).
@fill
@lu Hir Jackett ass [blo] a si huet eng Kaputz un.
@en Her jacket is [blue] and she is wearing a hood.
@distractor-lu giel
@distractor-lu donkel
@distractor-en yellow
@distractor-en dark

# The visible-state frame reused for the second child, with the opposite
# state to the earlier `[kal]` blank — both are checkable against the photo's
# two children, so the pair together teaches that the same frame can carry
# either answer depending on which person is described.
@fill
@lu D'Kand niewent hir gesäit [glécklech] aus.
@en The child next to her looks [happy].
@distractor-lu midd
@distractor-lu traureg
@distractor-en tired
@distractor-en sad

# The relative-clause frame reused a second time with a different subject and
# verb — a girl who has something on, rather than a man who is doing something.
# The comma ends the clause, keeping the blank after `Meedchen` safe under the
# Eifeler Regel; the blank is the garment noun, and `eng` fixed before it rules
# out masculine tiles on grammar (R4) as well as on the photo.
@fill
@lu Ech gesinn e Meedchen, dat eng [Jackett] un huet.
@en I see a girl who has a [jacket] on.
@distractor-lu Mantel
@distractor-lu Kaputz
@distractor-en coat
@distractor-en hood

# The `hunn … un` clothing frame a third time, now for the trousers rather than
# the jacket, and with the plural subject `d'Männer` making the plural verb form
# the correct tile — the singular distractor fails on agreement (R3) as well as
# the two masculine-noun distractors failing on gender (R4).
@fill
@lu D'Männer [hunn] hell Box un.
@en The men [have] light trousers on.
@distractor-lu huet
@distractor-lu Mantel
@distractor-en has
@distractor-en coat
